1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
My name is Shivam Gupta, and this is AFTERHOURS: The Tenth Prescription.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:17,000
The pharmacy closed ten years ago. Tonight, its lights came back on. Your
light reveals sealed names, but it is also what keeps the shadows back.

3
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:33,670
Explore each ward, reveal three prescriptions with Space, then collect them
with E. The same pulse stuns nearby shadows. Every pulse costs battery, so
recovering a name and protecting yourself become the same difficult
decision.

4
00:00:33,670 --> 00:00:39,670
Bring every record to the hatch, and another piece of the pharmacy's story
returns.

5
00:00:39,670 --> 00:00:44,670
Each of the ten authored wards introduces a new survival rule.

6
00:00:44,670 --> 00:01:00,870
Here, the alarm draws the shadows toward you. Watch the countdown and plan
your route. Quieter movement, hiding places and charging stations give you
options beyond the route shown here. Safety always comes with a decision.

7
00:01:00,870 --> 00:01:06,870
The records are not a shopping list. They preserve people the world has
forgotten.

8
00:01:06,870 --> 00:01:11,870
English and Korean carry the same story, objectives and survival
instructions.

9
00:01:11,870 --> 00:01:26,600
In this later ward, timed darkness and hazardous residue change familiar
routes. Light still reveals the way forward. The full game combines these
threats across ten distinct layouts.

10
00:01:26,600 --> 00:01:37,600
Recovered memories stay in your archive. Your sister, the last pharmacist,
refused to let these names disappear. Local progress lets you return to
finish what she began.

11
00:01:37,600 --> 00:01:42,600
This is a preview of the final choice: what should the pharmacy remember?

12
00:01:42,600 --> 00:01:52,600
The jam version includes all ten wards. An expanded premium game is a future
hypothesis, shaped by player feedback, rather than a promise of proven
demand.

13
00:01:52,600 --> 00:01:59,833
AFTERHOURS asks one question: when someone disappears from the records, who
keeps the light on?
